=== Major legislation ===
{{See also|List of acts of the 54th New Zealand Parliament}}

====2023–2024====
In December 2023, the Government repealed several of the previous [[Sixth Labour Government of New Zealand|Labour Government]]'s legislation and policies including the [[Reserve Bank of New Zealand]]'s dual mandate, the [[Fair Pay Agreements Act 2022]], the [[Ministry of Transport (New Zealand)#Clean Car Discount|Clean Car Discount]] programme, the [[Natural and Built Environment Act 2023]] and the [[Spatial Planning Act 2023]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Coughlan |first1=Thomas |title=New Government passes its first bill, reverting Reserve Bank to single mandate |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/new-government-passes-its-first-bill-reverting-reserve-bank-to-single-mandate/PUIWN7KPURB75PXELR4DTBHQIU/ |access-date=15 December 2023 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=13 December 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231213163451/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/new-government-passes-its-first-bill-reverting-reserve-bank-to-single-mandate/PUIWN7KPURB75PXELR4DTBHQIU/ |archive-date=13 December 2023}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Parliament repeals Fair Pay Agreements |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/504707/parliament-repeals-fair-pay-agreements |access-date=14 December 2023 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=14 December 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231214095111/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/504707/parliament-repeals-fair-pay-agreements |archive-date=14 December 2023|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Government repeals Clean Car Discount |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/301027372/government-repeals-clean-car-discount |access-date=15 December 2023 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=14 December 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231214063353/https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/301027372/government-repeals-clean-car-discount |archive-date=14 December 2023|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Bishop |first1=Chris |title=NBA and SPA successfully repealed |url=https://www.beehive.govt.nz/release/nba-and-spa-successfully-repealed |website=Beehive.govt.nz |publisher=[[New Zealand Government]] |access-date=21 December 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231220093404/https://www.beehive.govt.nz/release/nba-and-spa-successfully-repealed |archive-date=20 December 2023 |date=20 December 2023 |url-status=live}}</ref> On 21 December, the Government passed legislation reinstating 90-day work trials.<ref>{{cite news |title=Government passes legislation to fully reinstate 90-day trials |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/505252/government-passes-legislation-to-fully-reinstate-90-day-trials |access-date=21 December 2023 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=21 December 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231221013911/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/505252/government-passes-legislation-to-fully-reinstate-90-day-trials |archive-date=21 December 2023|url-status=live}}</ref> In February 2024, the Government repealed the [[Three Waters reform programme]], [[Smokefree Environments and Regulated Products (Smoked Tobacco) Amendment Act 2022]] and disestablished [[Te Aka Whai Ora]] (the Māori Health Authority) under urgency.<ref>{{cite news |title=Parliament repeals Three Waters programme under urgency |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/509144/parliament-repeals-three-waters-programme-under-urgency |access-date=18 February 2024 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=16 February 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240216020717/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/509144/parliament-repeals-three-waters-programme-under-urgency |archive-date=16 February 2024}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Harawira |first1=Tumamao |title=Parliament passes bill to disestablish Te Aka Whai Ora |url=https://www.teaonews.co.nz/2024/02/28/parliament-passes-bill-to-disestablish-te-aka-whai-ora/ |access-date=3 March 2024 |work=[[Te Ao Māori News]] |publisher=[[Whakaata Māori]] |date=28 February 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240303101758/https://www.teaonews.co.nz/2024/02/28/parliament-passes-bill-to-disestablish-te-aka-whai-ora/|archive-date=3 March 2024|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Smokefree generation law scrapped by coalition government |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/510439/smokefree-generation-law-scrapped-by-coalition-government |access-date=1 March 2024 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=28 February 2024 |archive-date=29 February 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240229155751/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/510439/smokefree-generation-law-scrapped-by-coalition-government |url-status=live }}</ref>

In late March 2024, the Government passed major tax legislation restoring interest deductibility for residential investment property, reducing the bright-line test for residential property to two years, and eliminating depreciation deductions for commercial and industrial buildings.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Willis |first1=Nicola |last2=Watts |first2=Simon |title=Passage of major tax bill welcomed |url=https://www.beehive.govt.nz/release/passage-major-tax-bill-welcomed |website=Beehive.govt.nz |publisher=[[New Zealand Government]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240327225344/https://www.beehive.govt.nz/release/passage-major-tax-bill-welcomed |archive-date=27 March 2024 |date=27 March 2024 |url-status=live}}</ref> The Government also passed legislation requiring [[electric car]]s and [[plug-in hybrid]]s to pay road user charges.<ref>{{cite news |title=EV and plug-in hybrid RUC legislation |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/512934/ev-and-plug-in-hybrid-ruc-legislation-passes-through-parliament |access-date=2 April 2024 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=28 March 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240331171512/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/512934/ev-and-plug-in-hybrid-ruc-legislation-passes-through-parliament |archive-date=31 March 2024|url-status=live}}</ref> In April 2024, the Government passed the [[Misuse of Drugs (Pseudoephedrine) Amendment Act 2024|legislation]] allowing 11 [[pseudoephedrine]] cold and flu medicines to be sold without prescriptions from June 2024.<ref>{{Cite news|title=Pseudoephedrine cold and flu products back on shelves this winter |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2024/04/12/pseudoephedrine-cold-and-flu-products-back-on-shelves-this-winter/ |access-date=12 April 2024 |date=12 April 2024|work=[[1News]]|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240415014840/https://www.1news.co.nz/2024/04/12/pseudoephedrine-cold-and-flu-products-back-on-shelves-this-winter/|archive-date=15 April 2024|language=en|url-status=live}}</ref>

In late July 2024, the Government passed [[Local Government (Electoral Legislation and Māori Wards and Māori Constituencies) Amendment Act 2024|legislation]] reinstating the referendum requirement for [[Māori wards and constituencies]] in local councils. Councils that had previously established a Māori ward without a referendum are now required to hold a binding poll alongside the [[2025 New Zealand local elections]] or to disestablish them.<ref name="RNZ 30 Jul 2024">{{cite news |title=Māori wards bill passes third reading |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/te-manu-korihi/523622/maori-wards-bill-passes-third-reading |access-date=30 July 2024 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=30 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240730095311/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/te-manu-korihi/523622/maori-wards-bill-passes-third-reading |archive-date=30 July 2024|url-status=live}}</ref> In late August 2024, the Government passed [[Local Water Done Well#Local Government (Water Services Preliminary Arrangements) Act 2024|legislation]] requiring local councils to develop plans for delivering drinking water, wastewater and stormwater services as part of its "[[Local Water Done Well]]" programme.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Dexter |first1=Giles |title=Bill for local council water service delivery passes third reading |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/526349/bill-for-local-council-water-service-delivery-passes-third-reading |access-date=1 September 2024 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=28 August 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241227191153/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/526349/bill-for-local-council-water-service-delivery-passes-third-reading|archive-date=27 December 2024|url-status=live}}</ref>

In September 2024, Parliament passed a private member's bill amending the [[Fair Trading Act 1986]] to ensure that gift cards have a minimum expiry date of three years from their initial purchase. The bill was supported by all parties except [[ACT New Zealand|ACT]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Dexter |first1=Giles |title=Parliament votes to extend gift card expiry dates |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/527716/parliament-votes-to-extend-gift-card-expiry-dates |access-date=11 September 2024 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=11 September 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240911130147/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/527716/parliament-votes-to-extend-gift-card-expiry-dates |archive-date=11 September 2024|url-status=live}}</ref> In mid October 2024, Parliament passed [[Deborah Russell]]'s private member's bill exempting victims of domestic violence from waiting a mandatory two years to seek a divorce.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Graham-McClay |first1=Charlotte |title=New Zealand exempts abuse victims from a forced two-year wait before they can seek divorce |url=https://apnews.com/article/divorce-zealand-marriage-domestic-abuse-violence-women-42f9d84eb0706f43e4cd9ec199fc5024 |access-date=16 October 2024 |work=[[Associated Press]] |date=11 October 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241223200531/https://apnews.com/article/divorce-zealand-marriage-domestic-abuse-violence-women-42f9d84eb0706f43e4cd9ec199fc5024|archive-date=23 December 2024|url-status=live}}</ref> In late October 2024, National-led government passed the legislation easing the "regulatory burden" on the country's farming, mining and other primary industries.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Collins |first1=Louis |title=The House: Government passes Resource Management Act changes to freshwater |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/thehouse/531811/the-house-government-passes-resource-management-act-changes-to-freshwater |access-date=25 October 2024 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=24 October 2024}}</ref> In late November 2024, Parliament with cross-party support passed [[Citizenship (Western Samoa) (Restoration) Amendment Act 2024|legislation]] restoring [[New Zealand nationality law|New Zealand citizenship]] to people born in [[Samoa]] between 1924 and 1949, who had been deprived of New Zealand citizenship in 1982.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Tinetali-Fiavaai |first1=Grace |title='685's to the world:' Restoring Citizenship Bill passes with unanimous support |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/international/pacific-news/534339/685-s-to-the-world-restoring-citizenship-bill-passes-with-unanimous-support |access-date=20 November 2024 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=20 November 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241120085644/https://www.rnz.co.nz/international/pacific-news/534339/685-s-to-the-world-restoring-citizenship-bill-passes-with-unanimous-support|archive-date=20 November 2024|url-status=live}}</ref>

On 10 December 2024, Parliament passed  urgent legislation banning [[greyhound]] dogs from being killed while the Government took action to end [[greyhound racing in New Zealand]] by July 2026.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Morrah |first1=Michael |title=Winston Peters announces greyhound racing ban to protect dog welfare |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/winston-peters-announces-greyhound-racing-ban-to-protect-dog-welfare/WOGNW5WPHBHSPPWT7RYXMHIAXI/ |access-date=13 December 2024 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=10 December 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241211171538/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/winston-peters-announces-greyhound-racing-ban-to-protect-dog-welfare/WOGNW5WPHBHSPPWT7RYXMHIAXI/ |archive-date=11 December 2024}}</ref> On 12 December, Parliament passed the government's legislation introducing its pet bonds for tenants and reinstating 90 day no-cause evictions.<ref>{{cite news |title=Pet bonds, no-cause evictions legislation passes |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2024/12/12/pet-bonds-no-cause-evictions-legislation-passes/ |access-date=16 December 2024 |work=[[1 News]] |date=12 December 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241216040739/https://www.1news.co.nz/2024/12/12/pet-bonds-no-cause-evictions-legislation-passes/|archive-date=16 December 2024|url-status=live}}</ref> On 13 December, Parliament passed the government's [[Sentencing (Reinstating Three Strikes) Amendment Act 2024|legislation]] reinstating [[three-strikes law]]s.<ref name="NZH 13 Dec 2024">{{cite news |title=Three Strikes legislation passes final hurdle in Parliament |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/three-strikes-legislation-passes-final-hurdle-in-parliament/2EQ5LEP6L5CEFGSZVN64FVHETI/ |access-date=17 December 2024 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=13 December 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241216041512/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/three-strikes-legislation-passes-final-hurdle-in-parliament/2EQ5LEP6L5CEFGSZVN64FVHETI/ |archive-date=16 December 2024}}</ref> On 17 December, the Government's contentious [[Fast-track Approvals Act 2024]] passed into law.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Ensor |first1=Jamie |title=Fast-track Approvals Bill passes final reading in Parliament |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/fast-track-approvals-bill-passes-final-reading-in-parliament/ROQWYD26IFA2RNGORGFLDDWFJA/ |access-date=21 December 2024 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=17 December 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241220022011/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/fast-track-approvals-bill-passes-final-reading-in-parliament/ROQWYD26IFA2RNGORGFLDDWFJA/ |archive-date=20 December 2024}}</ref>

====2025====
On 30 January 2025, Parliament passed the Taranaki Maunga Collective Redress Bill, which conferred legal personhood on [[Mount Taranaki]]. The [[New Zealand Crown]] also apologised to eight Māori [[iwi]] for confiscating Mount Taranaki and 1.2 million acres of Māori lands in the [[Taranaki]] region. In addition, Mount Egmont would cease to be an official name for Mt Taranaki.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Andrews |first1=Emma |last2=Paewai |first2=Pokere |title=Taranaki Maunga becomes a legal person as treaty settlement passes into law |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/national/540431/taranaki-maunga-becomes-a-legal-person-as-treaty-settlement-passes-into-law |access-date=30 January 2025 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=30 January 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250130210906/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/national/540431/taranaki-maunga-becomes-a-legal-person-as-treaty-settlement-passes-into-law |archive-date=30 January 2025}}</ref>

On 12 March, Parliament passed Labour MP [[Camilla Belich]]'s Crimes (Theft By Employer) Amendment Bill, clarifying that an employer withholding an employee's wages is theft. While the bill was opposed by National and ACT (60 votes), it passed with the support of the Labour, Green, Māori and New Zealand First parties (63 votes). The bill received royal assent on 13 March.<ref>{{cite news |last=Palmer |first=Russell |date=13 March 2025 |title=Bill making employers theft of workers' wages a crime passes |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/544621/theft-is-theft-bill-making-employers-theft-of-workers-wages-a-crime-passes |access-date=13 March 2025 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]]|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250312203048/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/544621/theft-is-theft-bill-making-employers-theft-of-workers-wages-a-crime-passes|archive-date=12 March 2025|url-status=live}}</ref>

On 7 May, Parliament passed the [[Equal Pay Amendment Act 2025]] under urgency, raising the threshold for making pay equity claims. As a result, 33 claims representing thousands of workers have to be dropped and refiled. The bill was supported by the governing coalition but opposed by all opposition parties.<ref>{{cite news |title=Pay Equity Amendment Bill passes under urgency |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/560257/pay-equity-amendment-bill-passes-under-urgency |access-date=8 May 2025 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=8 May 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250508060210/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/560257/pay-equity-amendment-bill-passes-under-urgency |archive-date=8 May 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> On 15 May, Parliament passed legislation formalising a Treaty of Waitangi settlement with [[Ngāti Ranginui]]; which included NZ$38 million in financial restitution and returning 15 sites of historical significance to the tribe.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Paewai |first1=Pokere |title=Parliament passes Ngā Hapū o Ngāti Ranginui Claims Settlement Bill |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/te-manu-korihi/561139/parliament-passes-nga-hapu-o-ngati-ranginui-claims-settlement-bill |access-date=30 January 2026|work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=15 May 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250515081323/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/te-manu-korihi/561139/parliament-passes-nga-hapu-o-ngati-ranginui-claims-settlement-bill |archive-date=15 May 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref>

On 26 June, Parliament passed [[Oversight of Oranga Tamariki System Legislation Amendment Act 2025|legislation]] designating the [[Independent Children's Monitor]] as a stand-alone independent [[Crown entity]], disestablishing the [[Children and Young People's Commission]] and reinstating the [[Children's Commissioner (New Zealand)|Children's Commissioner]]; effective 1 August 2025. The bill was supported by all parties except Te Pāti Māori.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Dexter |first1=Giles |title=Legislation to improve independent monitoring of Oranga Tamariki passes third reading |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/565159/legislation-to-improve-independent-monitoring-of-oranga-tamariki-passes-third-reading |access-date=4 August 2025 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=26 June 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250711061543/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/565159/legislation-to-improve-independent-monitoring-of-oranga-tamariki-passes-third-reading |archive-date=11 July 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> On 31 July, Parliament passed [[Crown Minerals Amendment Act 2025|legislation]] repealing a 2018 law limiting new oil and gas exploration permits off the coast of [[Taranaki]] along party lines.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Collins |first1=Louis |title=Petroleum law passes, with a last minute twist |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/568607/petroleum-law-passes-with-a-last-minute-twist |access-date=31 July 2025 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=31 July 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250731061409/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/568607/petroleum-law-passes-with-a-last-minute-twist |archive-date=31 July 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref>

On 19 August, the Government passed two laws, the Local Government (Water Services) Bill and the Local Government (Water Services) (Repeals and Amendments) Bill, entrenching its [[Local Water Done Well]] framework.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hanly |first1=Lillian |title=Legislation to implement Three Waters replacement passes third reading |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/570445/legislation-to-implement-three-waters-replacement-passes-third-reading |access-date=21 August 2025 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=19 August 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250821011954/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/570445/legislation-to-implement-three-waters-replacement-passes-third-reading |archive-date=21 August 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> On 20 August, Parliament passed Labour MP [[Camilla Belich]]'s bill banning employers from imposing gag orders on workers talking about their salaries passed into law with the support of the Green, Māori, and National parties. That same day, Labour MP [[Tracey McLellan]]'s bill extending the range of protections for those giving evidence of sexual assaults or family harm in the [[Family Court of New Zealand|Family Court]] passed into law with unanimous support.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hanly |first1=Lillian |title=Two Labour bills pass into law |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/national/570603/two-labour-bills-pass-into-law |access-date=21 August 2025 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=21 August 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250821010442/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/national/570603/two-labour-bills-pass-into-law |archive-date=21 August 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref>

On 17 September, Parliament passed urgent legislation preventing convicted abusers from adopting children from other countries.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Pearse |first1=Adam |title=Govt scrambles to change law allowing convicted abusers to adopt internationally |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/govt-scrambles-to-change-law-allowing-convicted-abusers-to-adopt-internationally/KYLHRJA2QRGKTHIIZQAJY43O64/ |access-date=3 October 2025 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=16 September 2025}}</ref> On 14 October, Parliament passed legislation allowing radio and television stations to broadcast advertisements on [[Christmas Day]], [[Good Friday]], [[Easter Sunday]] and [[Anzac Day]]. The legislation was supported by the National, Labour and ACT parties but was opposed by the Greens, New Zealand First and Te Pāti Māori.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Currie |first1=Shayne |title=Media Insider: TV advertisements can now be screened on Sunday mornings, Christmas Day and other public holidays|url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/business/media-insider/media-insider-tv-advertisements-can-now-be-screened-on-sunday-mornings-christmas-day-and-other-public-holidays/PM2BGEILARCP3AW7D2SYUU5PA4/ |access-date=20 October 2025 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=14 October 2025|url-access=subscription}}</ref> On 22 October, Parliament passed the government's [[Marine and Coastal Area (Takutai Moana) (Customary Marine Title) Amendment Act 2025|legislation]] limiting the threshold for Māori [[New Zealand foreshore and seabed controversy|foreshore and seabed claims]]. Opposition parties vowed to repeal the legislation if they formed the next government.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Smith |first1=Anneke |title=Opposition promises to repeal marine and coastal rights law change |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/576583/opposition-promises-to-repeal-marine-and-coastal-rights-law-change |access-date=27 October 2025 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=22 October 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251027045150/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/576583/opposition-promises-to-repeal-marine-and-coastal-rights-law-change |archive-date=27 October 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref>

On 5 November, Parliament passed legislation formalizing a Treaty of Waitangi settlement with [[Ngāti Pāoa]]. The settlement includes NZ$23.5 million in financial compensation, recognising 12 "culturally significant" sites, and a formal Crown apology for historical land alienation.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Dunlop |first1=Māni |title=Ngāti Pāoa Treaty Settlement Bill passes final reading in Parliament |url=https://www.teaonews.co.nz/2025/11/05/ngati-paoa-treaty-settlement-bill-passes-final-reading-in-parliament/ |access-date=10 November 2025 |work=[[Te Ao Māori News]] |date=5 November 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251110044511/https://www.teaonews.co.nz/2025/11/05/ngati-paoa-treaty-settlement-bill-passes-final-reading-in-parliament/|archive-date=10 November 2025|url-status=live}}</ref> On 13 November, ACT leader and Deputy Prime Minister [[David Seymour]]'s contentious [[Regulatory Standards Act 2025]] passed its third reading in Parliament, becoming law.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Palmer |first1=Russell |title=Controversial Regulatory Standards Bill passes third reading |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/578793/controversial-regulatory-standards-bill-passes-third-reading |access-date=13 November 2025 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=13 November 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251113200831/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/578793/controversial-regulatory-standards-bill-passes-third-reading |archive-date=13 November 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref>

On 11 December, Parliament passed two urgent government laws delaying a ban on [[farrowing crate]]s by ten years and [[Fast-track Approvals Amendment Act 2025|legislation]] amending the [[Fast-track Approvals Act 2024]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Palmer |first1=Russell |title=Government's pig farming law changes passed through Parliament under urgency |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/581566/government-s-pig-farming-law-changes-passed-through-parliament-under-urgency |access-date=13 December 2025 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=11 December 2025|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20251213111206/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/581566/government-s-pig-farming-law-changes-passed-through-parliament-under-urgency|archive-date=13 December 2025|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Palmer |first1=Russell|title=Fast-track amendment passes under urgency, backtracks on controversial proposals|url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/581500/fast-track-amendment-passes-under-urgency-backtracks-on-controversial-proposals |access-date=13 December 2025 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=11 December 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251213111455/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/581500/fast-track-amendment-passes-under-urgency-backtracks-on-controversial-proposals|archive-date=13 December 2025|url-status=live}}</ref> On 17 December, Parliament passed [[Electoral Amendment Act 2025|electoral amendment legislation]] reducing the timeframe for voter registration to two weeks before election day, banning prisoners from voting and allowing larger anonymous donations.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Palmer |first1=Russell |title=Electoral Amendment Bill passes its third reading in Parliament |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/582069/electoral-amendment-bill-passes-its-third-reading-in-parliament |access-date=18 December 2025 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=17 December 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251217032844/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/582069/electoral-amendment-bill-passes-its-third-reading-in-parliament |archive-date=17 December 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> That same day, Parliament passed a bill implementing the [[Nelson Tenths]] settlement between the [[Te Tau Ihu Māori]] and the New Zealand Crown.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Gee |first1=Samantha |title=South Island Māori landowners to get more than 3000 hectares returned by Crown |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/582055/south-island-maori-landowners-to-get-more-than-3000-hectares-returned-by-crown |access-date=16 December 2025 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=17 December 2025}}</ref>

====2026====
On 29 January, Parliament passed legislation formalising the Crown's Treaty settlement with [[Ngāti Hāua]]; which includes NZ$19 million worth of financial redress and the return of 64 culturally-significant sites.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Paewai |first1=Pokere |title='Carry that legacy on': Ngāti Hāua celebrates Treaty settlement |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/585451/carry-that-legacy-on-ngati-haua-celebrates-treaty-settlement |access-date=30 January 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=29 January 2026 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20260130025617/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/585451/carry-that-legacy-on-ngati-haua-celebrates-treaty-settlement|archive-date=30 January 2026|url-status=live}}</ref> On 17 February, Parliament passed the Government's controversial [[Employment Relations Amendment Act 2026]] which tightened the criteria for personal grievances claims, created a "gateway test" for differentiating between employees and contractors, ended payouts for employees dismissed for "serious misconduct," and eliminated a 30-day rule extending automatic collective agreement terms to new employees.<ref>{{cite news |title=Govt's controversial employment relations bill passes third reading |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2026/02/17/govts-controversial-employment-relations-bill-passes-third-reading/ |access-date=18 February 2026 |work=[[1News]] |date=17 February 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260218020926/https://www.1news.co.nz/2026/02/17/govts-controversial-employment-relations-bill-passes-third-reading/ |archive-date=18 February 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> That same week, Parliament passed legislation extending [[Anzac Day]] recognition to all servicemen who had served New Zealand in conflicts, peacekeeping operations and other deployments.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Collins |first1=Loius |title=Booze, betting, and the right to banter: Bills this week |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/587093/booze-betting-and-the-right-to-banter-bills-this-week |access-date=21 February 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=17 February 2026}}</ref>

In mid-February 2026, [[Minister for Infrastructure (New Zealand)|Minister for Infrastructure]] [[Chris Bishop]] released the country's first "National Infrastructure Plan." This plan outlines 16 recommendations and 10 priorities for the next decade. Bishop said that the Government would be seeking cross-party support across Parliament for the plan.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Smith |first1=Anneke |title=New Zealand's first national infrastructure plan unveiled |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/587072/new-zealand-s-first-national-infrastructure-plan-unveiled |access-date=18 February 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=17 February 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260218015922/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/587072/new-zealand-s-first-national-infrastructure-plan-unveiled |archive-date=18 February 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> By mid-June 2026, the opposition Labour and Green parties agreed to support the bulk of the National Infrastructure Plan's provisions. The Government agreed to implement 13 of the 16 recommendations and four further actions including reviewing the land transport funding system, introducing legislation requiring government departments and [[Crown entity|Crown entities]] to develop long-term investment and asset management plans, requiring infrastructure provides to maintain updated data in the National Infrastructure Network and developing a professional standard for public sector leadership.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Palmer |first1=Russell |title=30-year 'Infrastructure Plan' backed by Parliament |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/598348/30-year-infrastructure-plan-backed-by-parliament |access-date=18 June 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=16 February 2026 }}</ref>

On 1 April 2026, Parliament passed Labour MP [[Kieran McAnulty]]'s member's bill allowing more premises open on [[Good Friday]], [[Anzac Day]] and [[Christmas Day]] to sell alcohol under normal license conditions, with the exception of liquors stores and supermarkets.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Dexter |first1=Giles |title=Bill to ease holiday alcohol restrictions passes final reading in Parliament |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/591281/bill-to-ease-holiday-alcohol-restrictions-passes-final-reading-in-parliament |access-date=2 April 2026 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=1 April 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260401201923/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/591281/bill-to-ease-holiday-alcohol-restrictions-passes-final-reading-in-parliament |archive-date=1 April 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> In early April 2026, Parliament passed legislation reducing the [[housing]] intensification target in Auckland by 32.5 percent from 2 million to 1.4 million homes.<ref>{{cite web |title=Resource Management (Auckland Housing) Amendment Act|url=https://environment.govt.nz/acts-and-regulations/legislation/resource-management-auckland-housing-amendment-act/ |access-date=12 April 2026|website=[[Ministry for the Environment (New Zealand)|Ministry for the Environment]] |date=7 April 2026}}</ref> On 2 April, Parliament passed legislation creating the legislative framework for phasing out [[greyhound racing in New Zealand]] by 1 August 2026 including creating a transitional agency and rehoming the dogs.<ref>{{cite news |title=Greyhound racing to end in August as bill passes |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/top/591384/greyhound-racing-to-end-in-august-as-bill-passes |access-date=2 April 2026 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=2 April 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260402100355/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/top/591384/greyhound-racing-to-end-in-august-as-bill-passes|archive-date=2 April 2026|url-status=live}}</ref> On 28 April, Parliament passed the Government's controversial legislation legalising online [[casino]]s along party lines.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Stock |first1=Rob |title=MPs approve controversial bill to allow online casinos |url=https://www.thepost.co.nz/business/360992094/mps-approve-controversial-bill-allow-online-casinos |access-date=9 May 2026 |work=[[The Post (New Zealand newspaper)|The Post]] |date=24 April 2026|url-access=subscription}}</ref>

On 13 May, Parliament passed legislation entrenching three Treaty settlements with three [[Hauraki iwi]] (tribes): [[Ngāti Hei]], [[Ngāti Tara Tokanui]] and [[Ngāti Rāhiri Tumutumu]].<ref>{{cite web |last1=Goldsmith |first1=Paul |title=Three long-awaited Hauraki iwi settlement bills pass final reading |url=https://www.beehive.govt.nz/release/three-long-awaited-hauraki-iwi-settlement-bills-pass-final-reading |website=Beehive.govt.nz |publisher=[[New Zealand Government]] |access-date=20 May 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260514025722/https://www.beehive.govt.nz/release/three-long-awaited-hauraki-iwi-settlement-bills-pass-final-reading |archive-date=14 May 2026 |date=13 May 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> On 27 May, Parliament passed the Government's legislation disestablishing the [[Ministry for the Environment (New Zealand)|Ministry for the Environment]] and transferring its statutory functions to the new [[Ministry for Cities, Environment, Regions and Transport]] (MCERT), effective 1 July.<ref>{{cite news |title=Ministry for the Environment officially scrapped in Parliament vote |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/596538/ministry-for-the-environment-officially-scrapped-in-parliament-vote |access-date=27 May 2026 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=27 May 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260527221528/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/596538/ministry-for-the-environment-officially-scrapped-in-parliament-vote|archive-date=27 May 2026|url-status=live}}</ref> On 29 May, Parliament passed the Government's legislation under urgency allowing the [[Ministry of Social Development (New Zealand)|Ministry of Social Development]] to use artificial intelligence to make decisions regarding people's welfare benefits.<ref>{{cite news |title=Govt lauds new law allowing AI to make benefit decisions |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2026/05/30/govt-lauds-new-law-allowing-ai-to-make-benefit-decisions/ |access-date=31 May 2026 |work=[[1News]] |agency=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=30 May 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260530105215/https://www.1news.co.nz/2026/05/30/govt-lauds-new-law-allowing-ai-to-make-benefit-decisions/ |archive-date=30 May 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref>

On 1 July, Parliament passed the Government's contentious legislation reversing several 2015 workplace safety legislative changes that were passed following the 2010 [[Pike River Mine disaster]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Dexter |first1=Giles |title=Health and safety bill passes third reading, Labour promises repeal |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/651085/health-and-safety-bill-passes-third-reading-labour-promises-repeal |access-date=14 July 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=1 July 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260701064026/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/651085/health-and-safety-bill-passes-third-reading-labour-promises-repeal |archive-date=1 July 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> On 28 July, Parliament passed the [[Arms Act 2026]], which replaced the [[Arms Act 1983]] as the regulatory framework for [[Gun law in New Zealand|firearms in New Zealand]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Biggest overhaul of gun regulations in decades passes into law |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/831103/biggest-overhaul-of-gun-regulations-in-decades-passes-into-law |access-date=7 August 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=29 July 2026}}</ref> On 29 July, Parliament passed the Government's Employment Leave Bill replacing the Holiday Act. The new law bases employees' annual and sick leave in direct proportion to their standard work hours.<ref>{{cite news |title=The major changes to annual and sick leave that have just become law |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/nz-news/361013093/parliament-passes-major-overhaul-annual-and-sick-leave |access-date=30 July 2026 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=30 July 2026}}</ref> On 30 July, Parliament passed the [[English Language Act 2026]], which designated the [[English language]] as one of New Zealand's three official languages.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Dexter |first1=Giles |title=English to become official language of NZ alongside Te Reo Māori and sign language |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/844056/english-to-become-official-language-of-nz-alongside-te-reo-maori-and-sign-language |access-date=30 July 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=30 July 2026}}</ref> 

On 6 August, Parliament passed the Crimes Amendment Bill, which amended the [[Crimes Act 1961]] by creating new offences for assaulting first responders and [[Sucker punch|coward punching]], raised penalties for [[shoplifting]] and [[human trafficking]], and expanded [[citizen's arrest]] powers.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Ball |first1=James |title=New assault, shoplifting and citizen’s arrest changes pass into law |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2026/08/06/new-assault-shoplifting-and-citizens-arrest-laws-pass-into-law/ |access-date=6 August 2026 |work=[[1News]] |date=6 August 2026}}</ref> On 18 August, Parliament unanimously passed legislation entrenching the [[Nelson Tenths]] settlement into law.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Natanahira |first1=Tuwhenuaroa |title='Nelson Tenths' bill passing finally settles 180-year land dispute |url= https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/te-ao-maori/1052404/nelson-tenths-bill-passing-finally-settles-180-year-land-dispute |access-date=19 August 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=18 August 2026}}</ref> That same day, Parliament passed the contentious Climate Change Response (Tort Liability) Amendment Bill, which prevents lawsuits against companies for climate changing-emissions under [[tort law]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hanly |first1=Lillian |title=Legislation to end climate lawsuits against companies passes third reading in Parliament |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/environment_climate/1056108/legislation-to-end-climate-lawsuits-against-companies-passes-third-reading-in-parliament |access-date=19 August 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=19 August 2026}}</ref>

On 3 September, Parliament passed the Emergency Management 2026, which replaced the Civil Defence Management Act 2002. <ref>{{cite web |title=Emergency Management Bill (No 2) |url=https://bills.parliament.nz/v/6/cdf180bd-f6c9-4242-1024-08de369d9192?lang=en&Tab=history |publisher=[[New Zealand Parliament]] |access-date=18 September 2026 |date=4 September 2026}}</ref>
That same day, Parliament passed legislation tightening the welfare benefits criteria for teenagers aged 18 and 19-years old based on a parental income test.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Upston |first1=Louise |title=Improvements made to welfare system |url=https://www.beehive.govt.nz/release/improvements-made-welfare-system |website=Beehive.govt.nz |publisher=[[New Zealand Government]] |access-date=18 September 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260830105049/https://www.beehive.govt.nz/release/improvements-made-welfare-system |archive-date=30 August 2026 |date=28 August 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> In mid-September, Parliament passed two separate bills enacting the Crown's settlements with [[Whanganui Māori]] and [[Ngāti Ruapani]].<ref>{{cite web |last1=Goldsmith |first1=Paul |title=Crown settles with NgāHapū o Te Iwi o Whanganui |url=https://www.beehive.govt.nz/release/crown-settles-ng%C4%81-hap%C5%AB-o-te-iwi-o-whanganui |publisher=[[New Zealand Parliament]] |access-date=18 September 2026 |date=3 September 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Ngati Ruapani mai Waikaremoana settlement: Tuai renamed, Crown apologises for attacks and land loss |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/gisborne-herald/news/ngati-ruapani-mai-waikaremoana-settlement-crown-apologises-for-attacks-and-land-loss/AUQXFQCVP5GNHBQJFFLQS5MMMA/ |access-date=18 September 2026 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |agency=[[Gisborne Herald]] |date=4 September 2026}}</ref> On 16 September, Parliament passed legislation directing [[Local government in New Zealand|local government bodies]] to focus on delivering core services and restricting council voting to elected members.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Watts |first1=Simon |title=Bill passed to get councils back on basics |url=https://www.beehive.govt.nz/release/bill-passed-get-councils-back-basics |website=Beehive.govt.nz |publisher=[[New Zealand Government]] |access-date=18 September 2026 |date=16 September 2026}}</ref> On 17 September, Parliament passed two separate laws implementing the [[New Zealand–India Free Trade Agreement]] and empowering the [[Youth Court of New Zealand]] to send youth offenders to [[Boot camps in New Zealand|Military Style Academies]].<ref>{{cite news |title=India free trade agreement edges closer after Parliament passes legislation |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/indonz/1429134/india-free-trade-agreement-edges-closer-after-parliament-passes-legislation |access-date=18 September 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=17 September 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Hanly |first1=Lillian |title=Bootcamps [sic] bill passes final reading under urgency, will become law |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/1446368/bootcamps-bill-passes-final-reading-under-urgency-will-become-law |access-date=18 September 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=18 September 2026}}</ref> 

On 18 September, Parliament passed the Government's contentious legislation empowering Police to issue "move-on" orders to people aged 18 years above for begging, rough sleeping, disorderly behaviour, disturbing the peace, and obstructing someone from entering a business.<ref>{{cite news |title='Move on, or face the consequences' – Move on orders pass its final reading |url=https://www.teaonews.co.nz/2026/09/18/move-on-or-face-the-consequences-move-on-orders-pass-its-final-reading/ |access-date=18 September 2026 |work=[[Te Ao Māori News]] |date=18 September 2026}}</ref> On 19 September, Parliament passed the government's legislation easing earthquake strengthening rules in most of New Zealand, particularly the upper [[North Island]] and the [[Chatham Islands]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Thousands of NZ buildings no longer require earthquake strengthening |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/1475293/thousands-of-nz-buildings-no-longer-require-earthquake-strengthening |access-date=23 September 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=19 September 2026}}</ref> On 22 September, Parliament passed the Government's [[Planning Act 2026]] and the [[Natural Environment Act 2026]], which will replace the [[Resource Management Act 1991]] as the main legislative framework for infrastructural and environmental regulations.<ref>{{cite news |title=RMA replacement laws pass in Parliament |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/1524236/rma-replacement-laws-pass-in-parliament |access-date=23 September 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=22 September 2026}}</ref>

===Workplace behaviour and disciplinary actions===
====Julie Anne Genter====
In early May 2024, [[Green Party of Aotearoa New Zealand|Green Party]] MP [[Julie Anne Genter]] was referred to Parliament's privileges committee following complaints that she intimidated [[New Zealand National Party|National Party]]'s MP [[Matt Doocey]] during a heated parliamentary exchange.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Trevett |first1=Claire |last2=Pearse |first2=Adam |title=Julie Anne Genter confrontation: Green MP speaks as Speaker refers her to Privileges Committee |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/julie-anne-genter-confrontation-speaker-to-decide-if-green-party-mp-will-face-privileges-committee/G3APOUJP3NHMRD4SLH4TWPA4UY/ |access-date=7 May 2024 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=7 May 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240507024623/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/julie-anne-genter-confrontation-speaker-to-decide-if-green-party-mp-will-face-privileges-committee/G3APOUJP3NHMRD4SLH4TWPA4UY/ |archive-date=7 May 2024|url-status=live}}</ref> In early August 2024, Genter was found in contempt of Parliament and ordered to apologise.<ref>{{cite news |title=Julie Anne Genter to be censured and apologise over shouting incident |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/523846/julie-anne-genter-to-be-censured-and-apologise-over-shouting-incident |access-date=1 August 2024 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=1 August 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240801002603/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/523846/julie-anne-genter-to-be-censured-and-apologise-over-shouting-incident |archive-date=1 August 2024|url-status=live}}</ref>

====Tākuta Ferris====
In late September 2024, [[Te Pati Māori]] MP [[Tākuta Ferris]] was referred to Parliament's Privileges Committee after he made remarks accused Members of Parliament of lying and obfuscation.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Desmarais |first1=Felix |title=Te Pāti Māori MP headed for Privileges Committee over 'lies' comments |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2024/09/25/te-pati-maori-mp-headed-for-privileges-committee-over-lies-comments/ |access-date=3 October 2024 |work=[[1News]] |date=25 September 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240928103224/https://www.1news.co.nz/2024/09/25/te-pati-maori-mp-headed-for-privileges-committee-over-lies-comments/ |archive-date=28 September 2024|url-status=live}}</ref> On 12 February 2025, the Privileges Committee found that Ferris deliberately misled the House and ordered that he apologise for calling other MPs "liars".<ref>{{cite news |last1=Ensor |first1=Jamie |title=Te Pāti Māori MP Tākuta Ferris 'deliberately misled' House, should apologise – committee |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/te-pati-maori-mp-takuta-ferris-deliberately-misled-house-required-to-apologise-committee/4IQFUUXH5ZH3JDYZH2WYJPILN4/ |access-date=13 February 2025 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=12 February 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250212081141/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/te-pati-maori-mp-takuta-ferris-deliberately-misled-house-required-to-apologise-committee/4IQFUUXH5ZH3JDYZH2WYJPILN4/#google_vignette |archive-date=12 February 2025}}</ref>

====2024 Treaty Principles Bill haka====
On 10 December 2024, Labour MP [[Peeni Henare]], Te Pāti Māori MPs [[Hana-Rawhiti Maipi-Clarke]], co-leaders [[Rawiri Waititi]] and [[Debbie Ngarewa-Packer]] were referred to the Privileges Committee for leading a [[haka]] (ka mate) that interrupted vote proceedings during the first reading of the [[Treaty Principles Bill]] on 14 November.<ref>{{cite news |last1=McConnell |first1=Glenn |title='Do I care? No': Te Pāti Māori responds to Speaker's rebuke over haka |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/politics/360517484/te-pati-maori-and-labour-mps-referred-privileges-committee-over-haka |access-date=15 December 2024 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=10 December 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241213013738/https://www.stuff.co.nz/politics/360517484/te-pati-maori-and-labour-mps-referred-privileges-committee-over-haka |archive-date=13 December 2024|url-status=live}}</ref> On 26 March, the Committee found that Henare had acted in a "disorderly" way in joining the Te Pāti Māori-led haka but ruled that his actions did not amount to "contempt."<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hanly |first1=Lillian |title=Labour's Peeni Henare not in contempt over 'disorderly' actions during Treaty Principles reading |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/547574/labour-s-peeni-henare-not-in-contempt-over-disorderly-actions-during-treaty-principles-reading |access-date=4 April 2025 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=26 March 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250326151950/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/547574/labour-s-peeni-henare-not-in-contempt-over-disorderly-actions-during-treaty-principles-reading |archive-date=26 March 2025|url-status=live}}</ref>

On 1 April 2025, Maipi-Clark, Waititi and Ngarewa-Packer declined to appear before the Privileges Committee, claiming they had been denied key legal rights such as a joint hearing, restrictions on their legal representation [[Christopher Finlayson]], expert testimony from [[tikanga]] (Māori culture) expert Tā Pou Temara denied, hearing scheduling conflicts being ignored and concerns about disciplinary action against Maipi-Clarke.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Natanahira |first1=Tuwhenuaroa |last2=Bailey-McDowell |first2=Layla |last3=Hanly |first3=Lillian |title=Te Pāti Māori MPs refuse to attend Parliament's Privileges Committee hearing over haka protest |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/556841/te-pati-maori-mps-refuse-to-attend-parliament-s-privileges-committee-hearing-over-haka-protest |access-date=3 April 2025 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=1 April 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250401033505/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/556841/te-pati-maori-mps-refuse-to-attend-parliament-s-privileges-committee-hearing-over-haka-protest |archive-date=1 April 2025|url-status=live}}</ref> On 2 April, Chairperson of Privileges Committee [[Judith Collins]] confirmed that the privileges hearing would go ahead regardless of whether the three TPM MPs turned up.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hanly |first1=Lillian |title=Privileges Committee hearing to go ahead without Te Pāti Māori MPs |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/556962/privileges-committee-hearing-to-go-ahead-without-te-pati-maori-mps |access-date=3 April 2025 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=2 April 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250403043953/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/556962/privileges-committee-hearing-to-go-ahead-without-te-pati-maori-mps |archive-date=3 April 2025}}</ref> In response, Ngarewa-Packer and Waititi announced that Te Pāti Māori would boycott the hearing and hold its own "alternative independent hearing," dismissing the Privileges Committee as a "[[kangaroo court]]."<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hanly |first1=Lillian |title=Te Pāti Māori to hold own hearing after boycotting Privileges Committee |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/te-manu-korihi/556984/te-pati-maori-to-hold-own-hearing-after-boycotting-privileges-committee |access-date=3 April 2025 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=3 April 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250403044403/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/te-manu-korihi/556984/te-pati-maori-to-hold-own-hearing-after-boycotting-privileges-committee |archive-date=3 April 2025}}</ref>

On 14 May, the Privileges Committee censured Maipi-Clark, Ngarewa-Packer and Waititi for "acting in a manner that could have the effect of intimidating a member of the House in the discharge of their duty" during the haka protest. Maipi-Clark was suspended from Parliament for seven days while the two co-leaders were suspended from Parliament for 21 days. The Privileges Committee's chair [[Judith Collins]] declined to confirm whether Parliamentary protocol would be updated to accommodate more [[Tikanga Māori]]. Te Pāti Māori issued a statement denouncing the verdict as punishment by colonial powers meant to intimidate them.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Burr |first1=Lloyd |title=Te Pati Maori receive 'harshest punishment Privileges Committee's ever handed out' |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/politics/360689225/te-pati-maori-receive-harshest-punishment-privileges-committees-ever-handed-out |access-date=14 May 2025 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=14 May 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250514095014/https://www.stuff.co.nz/politics/360689225/te-pati-maori-receive-harshest-punishment-privileges-committees-ever-handed-out |archive-date=14 May 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref>

On 20 May, Parliament held a debate on the Privileges Committee's recommendation to suspend three Te Pāti Māori MPs. Labour leader Chris Hipkins agreed that the three MPs broke the rules of Parliament but disagreed with the suspension, instead proposing that Waititi and Ngarewa-Packer be suspended from Parliament for 24 hours and that no further action be taken against Maipi-Clark. Privileges Committee chair Judith Collins defended the suspension, describing their conduct as a "serious incident." [[Leader of the House (New Zealand)|Leader of the House]] [[Chris Bishop]] successfully moved a motion that the suspension debate be deferred until 5 June, allowing the Te Pāti Māori MPs to participate in the upcoming debate around the [[2025 New Zealand budget]]. While the government coalition parties supported Bishop's motion, the opposition parties voted against it.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Gabel |first1=Julia |last2=Pearse |first2=Adam |last3=Ensor |first3=Jamie |title=Debate on Te Pāti Māori haka punishment delayed in shock Government move |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/uncertainty-remains-as-politicians-plan-for-lengthy-debate-over-te-pati-maori-punishment/Z3OE2XWDP5H23G6SSO4AE5BG7A/ |access-date=20 May 2025 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=20 May 2025}}</ref> On 5 June, Parliament voted along party lines to suspend the three TPM MPs.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Ng |first1=Kelly |title=Three Maori MPs suspended over 'intimidating' haka |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c5yg9k8x8pwo |access-date=5 June 2025 |work=[[BBC News]] |date=5 June 2025|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20250605124521/https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c5yg9k8x8pwo|archive-date=5 June 2025|url-status=live}}</ref>

====Green Party–New Zealand First tensions====
On 29 January 2025, the Green Party sent a letter asking Prime Minister [[Christopher Luxon]] and Speaker of the House [[Gerry Brownlee]] to condemn alleged racist and xenophobic remarks made by [[New Zealand First]] MPs and government ministers [[Winston Peters]] and [[Shane Jones]] towards several Green MPs from migrant backgrounds. Jones had made remarks about sending [[Mexicans]] home, a reference to Mexican-New Zealander MP [[Ricardo Menéndez March]] while Peters had accused Green MPs [[Lawrence Xu-Nan]] and [[Francisco Hernandez (politician)|Franciso Hernandez]] of seeking to impose "foreign ideas" on New Zealanders.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Smith |first1=Anneke |title=Greens ask PM to condemn 'outwardly racist' rhetoric |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/540293/greens-ask-pm-to-condemn-outwardly-racist-rhetoric |access-date=30 January 2025 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=29 January 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250129170525/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/540293/greens-ask-pm-to-condemn-outwardly-racist-rhetoric |archive-date=29 January 2025}}</ref> In response, Jones and Peters defended their remarks, with the former accusing the aforementioned foreign-born MPs of not respecting New Zealand culture and the latter accusing the Greens of "faux outrage."<ref>{{cite news |last1=Smith |first1=Sam |title=NZ First accuses Greens of 'pearl clutching' and 'faux outrage' as culture war hostilities escalate |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/politics/360564434/shane-jones-doubles-down-controversial-comments-calling-it-mexican-stand |access-date=30 January 2025 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=30 January 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250130205143/https://www.stuff.co.nz/politics/360564434/shane-jones-doubles-down-controversial-comments-calling-it-mexican-stand |archive-date=30 January 2025}}</ref> Luxon refused to confirm whether he would discipline Peters and Jones but advised other MPs to "watch their language." The Mexican Embassy to New Zealand said it had raised concerns about Peters and Jones' remarks through diplomatic channels. Peters subsequently confirm that he would meet with the Ambassador at [[Waitangi, Northland|Waitangi]] in early February.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Ensor |first1=Jamie |title=Mexican Embassy wades in after NZ First minister's comments: PM Christopher Luxon repeats 'rhetoric' warning |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/mexican-embassy-wades-in-after-nz-first-ministers-comments-pm-christopher-luxon-repeats-rhetoric-warning/C64ALTELQFF37AV25REJ2OU5RQ/ |access-date=30 January 2025 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=30 January 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250130205835/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/mexican-embassy-wades-in-after-nz-first-ministers-comments-pm-christopher-luxon-repeats-rhetoric-warning/C64ALTELQFF37AV25REJ2OU5RQ/ |archive-date=30 January 2025}}</ref>

On 19 February 2025, NZ First MPs Peters and Jones criticised Green MP Menéndez March for referring to New Zealand as "[[Aotearoa]]" while questioning [[Minister of Immigration (New Zealand)|Immigration Minister]] [[Erica Stanford]]. Peters also sought to change standing orders around references to New Zealand. Speaker Brownlee defended March's right to refer to New Zealand as Aotearoa but reminded MPs to refer to New Zealand by both its [[English language|English]] and [[Māori language]] names.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Dexter |first1=Giles |title=Winston Peters, Shane Jones again attack migrant Green MP Ricardo Menéndez March |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/542388/winston-peters-shane-jones-again-attack-migrant-green-mp-ricardo-menendez-march |access-date=22 February 2025 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=19 February 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250219215707/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/542388/winston-peters-shane-jones-again-attack-migrant-green-mp-ricardo-menendez-march |archive-date=19 February 2025}}</ref> On 4 March, Speaker Brownlee issued a ruling ordering Members of Parliament to stop complaining about the use of Aotearoa as the Māori name for New Zealand.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Armstrong |first1=Kathryn |title=Don't complain about use of New Zealand's Māori name, MPs told |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c743zqx453wo |access-date=6 March 2025 |work=[[BBC News]] |date=4 March 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250306002334/https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c743zqx453wo |archive-date=6 March 2025}}</ref>

On 18 February 2026, Winston Peters twice criticised Green MP [[Teanau Tuiono]] for referring to New Zealand by its [[Māori language|Māori]] name [[Aotearoa]] during a Question Time debate on New Zealand's climate aid policies to the Pacific. In response, Labour leader Chris Hipkins accused Peters of making racist remarks to the ethnic [[Cook Islander]] MP while Speaker Brownlee cautioned Peters against asking unacceptable questions."<ref>{{cite news |title=Chris Hipkins accuses Winston Peters of 'pure racism' in Parliament |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/587214/chris-hipkins-accuses-winston-peters-of-pure-racism-in-parliament |access-date=20 February 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=18 February 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260218212512/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/587214/chris-hipkins-accuses-winston-peters-of-pure-racism-in-parliament |archive-date=18 February 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> On 19 February, Brownlee ejected Shadow Leader of the House and Labour MP [[Kieran McAnulty]] from a House debate after the latter accused the former of showing "double standards" towards Peters over his treatment of Tuiono.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Pearse |first1=Adam |title=Labour MP Kieran McAnulty booted from the House over Speaker's handling of Winston Peters' 'racist' remark |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/labour-mp-kieran-mcanulty-booted-from-the-house-over-speakers-handling-of-winston-peters-racist-remark/MR2AVPBXJBHCJH7AFODN3MICJA/ |access-date=20 February 2026 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=19 February 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260219115343/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/labour-mp-kieran-mcanulty-booted-from-the-house-over-speakers-handling-of-winston-peters-racist-remark/MR2AVPBXJBHCJH7AFODN3MICJA/ |archive-date=19 February 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref>

On 30 July, Peters told Green MP [[Lawrence Xu-Nan]] to "[[go back to your own country]]" during a debate on the [[New Zealand government response to the COVID-19 pandemic|previous government's response to the COVID-19 pandemic]].<ref>{{Cite news|last=McConnell |first=Glenn |date=30 July 2026 |title=Winston Peters launches tirade against Chinese-Kiwi Green MP |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/politics/361013006/winston-peters-launches-tirade-against-chinese-kiwi-green-mp |access-date=26 August 2026 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |language=en}}</ref> His remarks drew criticism from other parliamentarians across the spectrum as well as the Chinese Ambassador Wang Xiaoling.<ref>{{cite news |last1=McCulloch |first1=Craig |title=Luxon criticises Winston Peters for ‘inappropriate attention-seeking comments" towards Green MP |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/842204/luxon-criticises-winston-peters-for-inappropriate-attention-seeking-comments-towards-green-mp |access-date=26 August 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=30 July 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=McCulloch |first1=Craig |title=China's ambassador weighs in as Winston Peters tells Green MP to 'go back to your own country' |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/837502/china-s-ambassador-weighs-in-as-winston-peters-tells-green-mp-to-go-back-to-your-own-country |access-date=26 August 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=30 July 2026}}</ref> Prime Minister Luxon condemned Peters' comments as "offensive" but declined to remove his as Foreign Minister.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Lynch |first1=Jenna |title=Luxon expressed confidence in Peters as foreign minister, says his remarks were racist|url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/politics/361014135/luxon-expresses-confidence-peters-foreign-minister-says-his-remarks-were-racist |access-date=26 August 2026 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=31 July 2026}}</ref> On 6 August, Speaker Brownlee rejected a Green Party request that Peters to be asked to withdraw and apologise for his remarks towards Xu-Nan.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hanly |first1=Lillian |title=Speaker will not take action over Winston Peters' 'racist' comments |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/903281/speaker-will-not-take-action-over-winston-peters-racist-comments |access-date=26 August 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=6 August 2026}}</ref>

====Chlöe Swarbrick====
On 12 August 2025, Greens co-leader [[Chlöe Swarbrick]] was removed from Parliament for the rest of the week by Speaker Brownlee after she criticised Government MPs during a debate on the [[International recognition of Palestine|recognition of Palestine]].<ref>{{Cite news |date=12 August 2025 |title=New Zealand politician removed from parliament following comments in Palestinian debate |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/asia-pacific/new-zealand-politician-removed-parliament-following-comments-palestinian-debate-2025-08-12/ |access-date=12 August 2025|agency=[[Reuters]] |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|last=Gabel |first=Julia |title=Watch: Swarbrick booted from House for rest of week over Palestine speech |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/chloe-swarbrick-booted-from-house-during-palestine-speech/I4DXHM5GGRDKJBXJ6MNTNFOGWA/ |url-access=subscription |date=12 August 2025|access-date=12 August 2025 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |language=en-NZ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250812073628/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/chloe-swarbrick-booted-from-house-during-palestine-speech/I4DXHM5GGRDKJBXJ6MNTNFOGWA/|archive-date=12 August 2025|url-status=live}}</ref> The following day, Swarbrick refused to apologise and was "[[Naming (parliamentary procedure)|named]]" by Brownlee, who ordered her to leave Parliament's debating chamber once again. This punishment means that Swarbrick as an MP will have to leave the House of Representatives once again and will have her pay docked for the duration of her suspension.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Palmer |first1=Russell |last2=Dexter |first2=Giles |title=Green Party co-leader Chlöe Swarbrick 'named' for refusing to leave Parliament |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/569863/green-party-co-leader-chloe-swarbrick-named-for-refusing-to-leave-parliament |access-date=14 August 2025 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=13 August 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250813062508/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/569863/green-party-co-leader-chloe-swarbrick-named-for-refusing-to-leave-parliament |archive-date=13 August 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref>

On 4 August 2026, Swarbrick was ejected from Parliament after accusing Speaker Brownlee of showing favoritism towards [[New Zealand First]] leader [[Winston Peters]] over opposition Members of Parliament during a Question Time debate. Swarbrick and her Green Party colleagues had also earlier condemned Peters for telling Green MP [[Lawrence Xu-Nan]] to "[[Go back to your own country|go back to his own country]]" during a parliamentary debate in late July 2026.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hanly |first1=Lillian |title=Chlöe Swarbrick kicked out of House after challenging Speaker's 'double standards' |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/883243/chloe-swarbrick-kicked-out-of-house-after-challenging-speaker-s-double-standards |access-date=5 August 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=4 August 2026}}</ref>

====MBIE biometric upgrades controversy====
On 26 August 2026, Parliament's Privileges Committee found two [[Ministry of Business, Innovation and Employment]] (MBIE) officials, chief executive Nick Blakeley and former [[Immigration New Zealand]] head Alison McDonald, in [[contempt of Parliament]] for not informing Parliament that a biometric upgrade project had been cancelled during a meeting with the Education and Workforce select committee in March 2026.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Pearse |first1=Adam |title=Public Service Commissioner refuses MBIE boss’ offer to resign after deliberately misleading MPs |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/officials-deliberately-misled-mps-over-failing-immigration-it-project-parliament-committee-rules/QE7M4Z6CUZFAXHE7DSQF2OWTQU/ |access-date=30 August 2026 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=26 August 2026 |url-access=subscription}}</ref> In late September 2026, Blakely acknowledged that MBIE had spent NZ$50.7 million in taxpayer funding on the botched biometric upgrade project without delivering any results.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Moir |first1=Jo |title=MBIE confirms botched biometric projects costs blown out to $50.7 million |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/1527356/mbie-confirms-botched-biometric-projects-costs-blown-out-to-50-point-7-million |access-date=28 September 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=23 September 2026}}</ref>

====Other ejections====
On 15 September 2026, Speaker Brownlee ordered Labour MP [[Ginny Andersen]] and [[ACT New Zealand|ACT]] leader [[David Seymour]] to leave the House of Representatives. Andersen had heckled National MP [[Carl Bates]] as he answered a question while Seymour had argued with the Speaker while questioning ACT MP [[Laura McClure]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Gabel |first1=Julia |title=Speaker Gerry Brownlee boots two MPs from House within minutes |url=https://www.newstalkzb.co.nz/news/politics/speaker-gerry-brownlee-boots-two-mps-from-house-within-minutes/ |access-date=18 September 2026 |work=[[Newstalk ZB]] |date=15 September 2026}}</ref>

On 17 September, [[Te Pāti Māori]] co-leader [[Debbie Ngarewa-Packer]] was ordered to leave the House after using the swear word "[[cunt]]" during an argument with Assistant Speaker [[Maureen Pugh]] during a debate on the National-led government's contentious move-on orders. Labour MP [[Willie Jackson (politician)|Willie Jackson]] was then ordered to leave after contesting Pugh's decision to discipline Ngarewa-Packer when the same word had previously been used in the House. After Jackson continued to speak, Pugh ordered the [[Serjeant-at-arms#New Zealand|Sergeant-at-arms]] to escort him from the debating chamber.<ref>{{cite news |title=Te Pāti Māori co-leader ejected from Parliament after c-word clash |url=https://www.teaonews.co.nz/2026/09/17/te-pati-maori-co-leader-ejected-from-parliament-after-c-word-clash/ |access-date=18 September 2026 |work=[[Te Ao Māori News]] |date=17 September 2026 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20260918043059/https://www.teaonews.co.nz/2026/09/17/te-pati-maori-co-leader-ejected-from-parliament-after-c-word-clash/ |archive-date=18 September 2026}}</ref>

=== Dissolution ===
The 54th Parliament will serve until another election is called. Under section 17 of the [[Constitution Act 1986]], Parliament expires three years "from the day fixed for the return of the writs issued for the last preceding general election of members of the House of Representatives, and no longer".<ref name="Constitution Act 1986">{{cite web |title=Constitution Act 1986 |url=http://www.legislation.govt.nz/act/public/1986/0114/latest/DLM94204.html |access-date=28 January 2020 |publisher=[[Parliamentary Counsel Office (New Zealand)|Parliamentary Counsel Office]]}}</ref> The writ for the [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023 election]] was issued on 10 September 2023 and returned on 16 November 2023, meaning that the 54th Parliament would have to dissolve on or before 16 November 2026.

