=== Overview ===
The table below shows the members of the 54th Parliament based on the results of the 2023 general election, including the result of the [[2023 Port Waikato by-election|Port Waikato by-election]]. Ministerial roles were officially announced on 24 November 2023.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Who gets what? List of New Zealand's new ministers |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2023/11/24/who-gets-what-list-of-new-zealands-new-ministers/ |access-date=24 November 2023 |website=1 News |language=en}}</ref>
Based on the official results, 41 candidates who had never been in parliament before were returned. Of those, 19 were from National,<ref name="National and Labour">{{cite news |date=17 October 2023 |title=Who are the new National and Labour MPs? |work=[[The Spinoff]] |url=https://thespinoff.co.nz/politics/17-10-2023/who-are-the-new-national-and-labour-mps |url-status=live |access-date=23 October 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231019021327/https://thespinoff.co.nz/politics/17-10-2023/who-are-the-new-national-and-labour-mps |archive-date=19 October 2023}}</ref> 2 from Labour,<ref name="National and Labour" /> 8 from the Greens,<ref name="Greens and Act">{{cite news |last1=Mathias |first1=Shanti |date=18 October 2023 |title=Who are the new Greens and Act MPs? |work=[[The Spinoff]] |url=https://thespinoff.co.nz/politics/18-10-2023/who-are-the-new-greens-and-act-mps |url-status=live |access-date=23 October 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231025024545/https://thespinoff.co.nz/politics/18-10-2023/who-are-the-new-greens-and-act-mps |archive-date=25 October 2023}}</ref> 4 from ACT,<ref name="Greens and Act" /> 4 from Te Pāti Māori,<ref>{{cite news |last1=de Silva |first1=Tommy |date=16 October 2023 |title=Meet the two new MPs from Te Pāti Māori |work=[[The Spinoff]] |url=https://thespinoff.co.nz/politics/16-10-2023/meet-the-two-new-mps-from-te-pati-maori |url-status=live |access-date=23 October 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231025032459/https://thespinoff.co.nz/politics/16-10-2023/meet-the-two-new-mps-from-te-pati-maori |archive-date=25 October 2023}}</ref> and 4 from NZ First.<ref>{{cite news |last1=de Silva |first1=Tommy |date=16 October 2023 |title=Meet the new NZ First MPs |work=[[The Spinoff]] |url=https://thespinoff.co.nz/politics/16-10-2023/meet-the-new-nz-first-mps |url-status=live |access-date=23 October 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231019225009/https://thespinoff.co.nz/politics/16-10-2023/meet-the-new-nz-first-mps |archive-date=19 October 2023}}</ref> The parliament totaled 123 seats after the conclusion of the Port Waikato by-election, meaning that one-third of the members are newcomers.

