=== Demographics ===
The 54th Parliament has a historically high number of [[Māori people|Māori]] MPs at 33. The number of female MPs, 55, is the second highest in New Zealand history, down from the high of 61 achieved during the 53rd Parliament.<ref name="who">{{cite web|url=https://i.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/301002369/whos-representing-you-record-for-mori-mps-but-fewer-women-in-parliament |title=Who's representing you? Record for Māori MPs, but fewer women in Parliament |access-date=9 November 2023}}</ref>

The number of [[Pasifika New Zealanders|Pasifika]] MPs, 6, is also down from the record number in the previous parliament, and is at its lowest number in 10 years.<ref name="who"/><ref>{{cite web|first1=Alexander |last1=Tan |first2=Neel |last2=Vanvari |url=https://theconversation.com/lost-voices-ethnic-diversity-in-the-new-zealand-parliament-will-decline-after-the-2023-election-217648 |title=Lost voices: Ethnic diversity in the New Zealand Parliament will decline after the 2023 election |publisher=The Conversation |date=16 November 2023 |access-date=24 November 2023}}</ref> There are currently no Pasifika MPs on the [[Sixth National Government of New Zealand|government]] benches.

Only 5 MPs who publicly identify as [[LGBT|LGBTQIA+]] were elected, 2 each from Labour and the Greens and 1 from ACT. This is down from a record 12 (10%) elected in the 2020 election.<ref>{{cite web|first1=Madeleine |last1=Chapman  |url=https://thespinoff.co.nz/politics/06-11-2023/a-national-act-nz-first-government-would-mean-a-whole-lot-of-men-in-charge-again |title=A National/Act/NZ First government would mean a whole lot of men in charge (again) |publisher=[[The Spinoff]] |date=6 November 2023 |access-date=24 November 2023}}</ref>

